# Compiled binaries. `go build` with no -o drops a binary named after the
# directory it built, which is how an 11MB `cmd` file once ended up
# committed-adjacent.
#
# These are deliberately anchored to a leading slash AND written without a
# trailing one, so each ignores a FILE at the repository root and never a
# directory. `/cmd` used to shadow the conventional Go `cmd/` source tree, so
# adding a second binary silently committed nothing and the mistake was
# invisible until someone went looking for the file they had written.
/job-hunter-toolkit
/job-hunter-mcp
*.exe
*.test

# Test and coverage output.
#
# Named exactly rather than by prefix. `coverage.*` was unanchored, so it
# matched any path segment beginning "coverage." anywhere in the tree -- and it
# silently swallowed internal/mcp/coverage.go, a source file, which built fine
# locally and failed CI with "undefined" errors for symbols whose definition was
# simply not in the repository. An ignore rule that can hide a .go file is worse
# than the artifact it was written for.
*.out
/coverage.html
/coverage.txt

# macOS
.DS_Store

# Assembled site (web/build.sh output); pages.yml builds it fresh.
web/dist/

# Repository-scoped Orb toolchains, caches, and temporary verification output.
.amp/
